;;; An `(absent …)` over a pattern with a wildcard `_` always holds —
;;; **W20**, register row `W20/wild-in-absent` (`corpus/wrong_answers.toml`),
;;; the case's mechanism under `absent`.
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; One fact, `(holds a)`, and one rule that concludes `(hit z)` while there
;;; is no `holds` fact at all. `holds` is closed (`:no-hypothesis`), so no
;;; commitment extends it and the `absent` keeps the program monotone.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; A bare `_` is reserved and names nothing (Q-M1e2.22), so the program is
;;; refused at the parse, at the `_`, on every command. Written `?any`, a
;;; variable the rule reads nowhere else (`wild-in-absent-twin.ein`),
;;; `(absent (holds ?any))` asks whether there is no `holds` fact, and
;;; `(holds a)` is one: `z` never fires, and R is `∅` and `{(hit a)}`.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered, and what it answers ──────────────────────
;;;
;;; At `c73a0796`, release and debug (every mode and `ein oracle` the same),
;;; `z` fired: `_` was an opaque slot that matched nothing, so the pattern
;;; under `absent` found no fact and the `absent` always held. A conclusion
;;; the program refutes was in every state: root held `(hit z)`, and R was
;;; `{(hit z)}` and `{(hit a), (hit z)}` (`ein solve FILE`). Today the parse
;;; refuses the `_`.
;;;

(relation holds Node) (relation hit Node)
(holds a)
(rule z () :match (absent (holds _)) :assert (hit z))
(solve :no-hypothesis (holds))
